=0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +8)-9(-16) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+37\\lambda -72)+144 & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+37\\lambda +72 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-37\\lambda -72 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f26ae68-d20b-562d-912a-a97d715dfe62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324479} {:answer/id #uuid "41fd81d6-bfea-5692-a214-fab06a9dd205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324505} {:answer/id #uuid "85332dfc-9589-5f1c-8a83-e6711279ec24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325095} {:answer/id #uuid "a4587bf1-4758-55bb-9682-e2e0dc9d5d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325369} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ca9124-4eba-575e-ace1-82e9c9ccbcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325095} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b03c312-2c19-56a2-a97f-baa7660ba4ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9f42611-c6f2-56b0-8496-f724b3d11a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42103" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{13}+1\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb923d4-b5d1-551e-bd0c-32922527b318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327043} {:answer/id #uuid "71821817-c757-531b-9efc-24a73efee7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327912} {:answer/id #uuid "80c4ef71-2974-507c-8dc4-cedd29fab1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 15 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328045} {:answer/id #uuid "b4108d0e-491e-58a6-a0d2-b874ac38c36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328499} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a77ed8-b41a-596b-9b96-7a5fd1cf8eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327043} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e7d9bc-21a9-572f-a899-701ed9839ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7c177d5-936c-5b91-b2dd-2e563024c669" :question/math-academy-id "q-42987" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${C}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 15 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $C$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $C$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 15 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-7){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 15 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${C}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd916e5-f9d5-53f7-abca-50eaccff2c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327330} {:answer/id #uuid "455613c8-bb09-540c-bb3b-c20aa221192e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327539} {:answer/id #uuid "c3359428-3dc0-5272-aa6c-515479db7b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328640} {:answer/id #uuid "d227293b-cecd-5e05-b7ca-90e76490a0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328780} {:answer/id #uuid "de8333d8-5f11-5e68-a87b-0f815d6b050f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328640} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd38e060-b522-5ef6-b8de-d36ed7fe5485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3138d68-b633-567e-bf04-14b148ac2a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43004" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $V$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1b6e1f-40a5-5d7b-8784-52be2d4ca406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327051} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0757fc-669d-5a05-92ec-a08c787dfe41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327315} {:answer/id #uuid "e14f5ab4-adf3-5429-9343-5b19271d44db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328904} {:answer/id #uuid "efa73263-ce8a-5673-93de-df606171424a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329027} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bdcfc9-f2d4-5cb8-871e-d5337b9f43b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329027} :answer-field/id #uuid "2268f6a5-26ee-5dac-8ee4-d4c7d3159727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce5531aa-6b05-5b32-a6bf-6f89f79abdc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-43005" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $Q$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${Q}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Q$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Q}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e7c760-83ed-5281-9a05-02bdd53f472e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451393} {:answer/id #uuid "1c25083c-8b7e-5fe5-8f5e-8eb45804062d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451394} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b8cf12-b1ee-56df-a82b-c4c4b8dbd00b" :answer-field/key "$det(A)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "695aba89-3993-5a92-98dc-4e9a55e876f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451395} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e90fc7-716b-51e9-bf6e-6eb941d76661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451395} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e267111-0c10-5c40-af31-2927630381d9" :answer-field/key "$Ax=0$ has no solution" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452545} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eb458c5-d305-5c0f-b510-c154501be818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451397} {:answer/id #uuid "37efc5e7-9dbf-570d-856e-a6d8bc5960e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451398} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6123ef-1037-5ebd-a9a2-fe6ad93895a1" :answer-field/key "The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5470569-5538-5c85-aec6-dfcb3a6e366a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47652" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has no solution” is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement “The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point” is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6943c140-8ce8-5ef3-a24a-ec4dd3989d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467584} {:answer/id #uuid "f903da43-b955-557b-b07e-9a56a9cb9411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467585} :answer-field/id #uuid "131a190d-2e93-5c45-8f99-0ed790058e01" :answer-field/key "$det(A)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a3ed0f-3e98-54bf-9262-acf83b1ca310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467586} {:answer/id #uuid "ce418b35-cc2f-53d8-bacf-bc6a8f351d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467587} :answer-field/id #uuid "71011c6f-48e1-5eb7-add1-32fab707cd37" :answer-field/key "$A$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801e1932-e9a9-5a2e-a418-1e17c385bb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467588} {:answer/id #uuid "6016d1b1-b380-5755-a999-dcec788a69fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467588} :answer-field/id #uuid "559e34e0-cb19-5844-a24f-bc87f0e1438e" :answer-field/key "${A}^{-1}$ does not exist" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffbb2a4c-595d-59cc-bffd-a39a18b105b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47657" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ .*\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options $det(A)\\ne 0$ and “$A$ is invertible” give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249b14f1-acb1-5636-9821-2061de94f93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333329} {:answer/id #uuid "37d89106-b06e-5743-9c8a-2b404d701fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333504} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc6c969-5272-5477-a7cd-77d273d1a347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333570} {:answer/id #uuid "ca870be6-c1ba-575f-b695-3daf4170cc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334753} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c1bf4e-922d-54da-8042-2f6e791b52e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335132} :answer-field/id #uuid "367e02b4-0093-589e-afe1-872cce74ea71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf461bb2-5354-5d4c-a70d-475dbba2d043" :question/math-academy-id "q-69952" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab1084c2-7b20-586c-8b1f-99049d33dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337574} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b8aca7-797c-5e23-a462-ed64d5d08ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337916} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b10e53-6ae7-5bca-9c43-2013f32f8bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338047} {:answer/id #uuid "f278f02b-3d15-5941-9084-d3627cb1966a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338155} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4559cd-3151-508b-aa9b-1533381d8843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338240} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9102b8-ee06-590b-84f9-daac11661942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d86e0666-0736-5e3f-b30a-8fb51d87b91a" :question/math-academy-id "q-69974" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08629f1e-c5d2-5f81-a5e9-5c9e466b1f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336102} {:answer/id #uuid "18da98e4-38dc-51ab-83d5-b67960d79dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336265} {:answer/id #uuid "214a22fa-c0d4-5880-a3b5-7190b59080b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336343} {:answer/id #uuid "7644a866-0487-5361-bb17-5ef5e9a87fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337097} {:answer/id #uuid "f148a18a-cb53-5e47-a600-44fc81b1522e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338144} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c5f631-3543-5c10-a4d1-d4d6fb576326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad21c558-ae5a-5895-ba39-892c87b1ad75" :question/math-academy-id "q-70108" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+1\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 5=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b353273-335f-5780-afb0-be75dcae7075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336283} {:answer/id #uuid "2d120b04-e055-5b9e-9000-4eb3aaf2ce01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336459} {:answer/id #uuid "40d52219-9a22-5340-9d52-4fb05d2cbce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336640} {:answer/id #uuid "484a4e8b-6df4-53f0-bacc-40a4d9dbcd63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336702} {:answer/id #uuid "67b19c38-9273-5308-a318-07e5af5d7a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336958} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaf0ba2-3df2-59a1-a7fc-b27211f32d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b33eca9-2a94-5443-8eea-bc482a963164" :question/math-academy-id "q-70109" :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =4\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(9-2) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=-4\\cdot (-14)=56.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000e1d96-8935-52bc-b473-92fb6b2a631c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336029} {:answer/id #uuid "66ca18d6-9c9c-5fe7-81e8-baffadbc3312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336949} {:answer/id #uuid "784268b3-b15a-510d-b906-29f354e1b2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337114} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4e359d-2c20-5e25-a85b-2c50b2ca090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337144} {:answer/id #uuid "e28197d9-4a50-5427-a602-17b7dcaf663c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337114} :answer-field/id #uuid "496c21df-6120-50a3-8f23-74c46cc96ee2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e26acf58-9de8-5792-a31a-d5cce9613711" :question/math-academy-id "q-70128" :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $2$nd row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23}+1\\cdot {C}_{24} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot (-1)^{2+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(6-2)-(-6-2) \\\\  & =-8-(-8) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+1\\cdot {C}_{12}+2\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(2)\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-6-2)+2(0-6) \\\\  & =-(-8)+2\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =8-12 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=-0+(-4)=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ac0f97-eeb8-5783-936b-1cb59033a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} {:answer/id #uuid "4b236f4f-c6e5-553f-9345-4b6a8bba7c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336725} {:answer/id #uuid "6029e681-c7c2-5a8b-96eb-6ad30cbe83ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336903} {:answer/id #uuid "72576609-7b8d-50eb-8ffa-8aef7ffc7d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337056} {:answer/id #uuid "862a7584-abcb-5134-9117-8e928e486ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336679} :answer-field/id #uuid "907db199-a612-5898-a313-2594bace9c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa886c44-6fbc-583a-a5aa-8d0e7dbb66ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-70133" :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $4$th row of the given matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So, using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+3\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$  determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{21}+2\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2-3)+2(1-3) \\\\  & =-5+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along its $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+1\\cdot {C}_{32}+1\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(4-2)+(-2+1) \\\\  & =-2+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=-2\\cdot (-9)+3\\cdot (-3)=18-9=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03087a9c-7c81-54df-8865-721b36cca8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339150} {:answer/id #uuid "46131c51-26ce-5ae9-bbec-cd76141b268d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339751} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1dacb8-dd5f-529b-a0ea-e1deea46ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340091} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8da1b5-c398-579a-aea4-b89e242441c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340270} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fb3893-e722-5a06-a2cf-16fddab1d09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339751} :answer-field/id #uuid "217eb4c8-abdc-5a9d-9700-26248de988b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef160bb4-443b-58b5-915b-9dfda073ef96" :question/math-academy-id "q-72729" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3y \\\\ 2x &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=0 \\\\ 2x &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cae29bf-6657-5948-b4ac-de9d15a83b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339243} {:answer/id #uuid "1c79ef91-d60f-5461-9182-a09de75ab44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339373} {:answer/id #uuid "50658568-e9ab-5946-9ac1-0eb64885c225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339861} {:answer/id #uuid "7a038809-9690-59a5-9f4d-8538600ea5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340216} {:answer/id #uuid "af86119f-4c68-5d25-8752-90c8805840da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340730} :answer-field/id #uuid "39ce73d2-08ab-546f-9ccf-5116afbbd6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae55f4eb-c009-586b-a5eb-f77f0f124cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72733" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=y+1 \\\\ x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a855b3fc-a58c-5d42-a023-aa1cf84fddf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340656} {:answer/id #uuid "b0524785-8288-5abb-802a-012c402c2c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340735} {:answer/id #uuid "c879817f-f47b-5f8c-bd26-909387da2146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340904} {:answer/id #uuid "cd44ee7c-1bb6-5ae0-982d-0157107836dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340948} {:answer/id #uuid "f3324b52-7891-5d54-8104-c33959c84d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340656} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a776a82-f7c0-52f5-ae5f-436886fab53b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc0bf99b-722a-5c69-a1c9-0059727d8fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72738" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x &=3y \\\\ y &=-x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x-3y &=0 \\\\ x+y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb2f9c1-c0f7-5cb1-94cf-c1eda0965c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451585} {:answer/id #uuid "fb399ecd-7930-593b-83c0-38b52228b9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451586} :answer-field/id #uuid "a104f9c7-7e4d-5227-b5d9-cb72664cf694" :answer-field/key "$det(A)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec2e54f-4842-5a73-9901-8fa041c1a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451587} {:answer/id #uuid "7460347d-b166-5979-bcfc-69ae5393c4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451587} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdfa12c5-bb59-5c70-9e62-1ffe6ff85434" :answer-field/key "$A$ is not invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f7cb3cb-e223-5f6f-b2d5-80b3571e9e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451589} {:answer/id #uuid "5475d51e-e019-56ab-8cdc-1e208b5f796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451589} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6537431-c15f-5384-9d0f-e746bb1bc7d8" :answer-field/key "The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6905816-c0d6-59f0-9ff1-518732f97672" :question/math-academy-id "q-73562" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f8db18-4936-5d58-b7eb-8e4f05c46d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451591} {:answer/id #uuid "c921bd68-ad9c-592c-9800-47bf22685ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451591} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e23e151-69ab-5150-a6dd-dddffad11cdb" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e5e15f-8163-5919-96fd-e0ad4f34643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451593} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b68a52-378f-5960-b347-47f6ca687d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451594} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa3ed8d-d8dc-50ef-a617-8f9288b9d4b0" :answer-field/key "The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5854a3-4400-582b-bf8c-6ff607f43d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451595} {:answer/id #uuid "c1601bb5-6b85-53dc-a4db-7a5cfa40df0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451596} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9234a48-131e-5b5b-beae-659f6d2f37fe" :answer-field/key "$A$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c8cd48-ec61-5529-9409-02950b9c8c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73563" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution” is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements “The system $Ax=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution”  and “$A$ is invertible” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60635149-f2da-567f-9f0a-9d4117894692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451597} {:answer/id #uuid "d830d804-449e-5e28-85c1-6748faf8d484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451598} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e139a64-7bda-5908-828b-d778f0b7810e" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58ffdb3c-5c54-5de6-884b-cb4ea0bf2b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451599} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f9328e-0820-5a7e-a411-86187b35cbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451599} :answer-field/id #uuid "358dbeef-add1-5d3c-974e-66d81b7957e3" :answer-field/key "The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c76fc4-db40-5d9f-b744-f4e8311ffe23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451601} {:answer/id #uuid "669f7e2b-36da-5c77-9bf8-b929a0f8fb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451602} :answer-field/id #uuid "3286acfd-ad75-5a43-83b7-7026982ba30d" :answer-field/key "${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b4e1216-e41e-5317-bf20-2acb3db54b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73565" :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point” is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements $det(A)=0$ and “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e722ed3-73e4-52c0-a421-554b9e9509ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451603} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8c1a33-14d5-5cc1-8cc8-cfa132ee5905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451603} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f67ecc6-4e68-5ff5-857f-036ed453fead" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6115e85e-49af-5de4-9017-fbc84811fcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451605} {:answer/id #uuid "eaebde56-3eac-5bbe-8241-b7a00be555c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9389fb0c-e8ff-5dd4-bcab-a96185abbeb4" :answer-field/key "The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa797b64-c1e0-5eb2-85fb-866fba1ac56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451607} {:answer/id #uuid "896ae4a2-9a84-5c01-a7e8-af08641f9223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451608} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b24a35-e5b9-5f93-a09d-4ba2dac53cd5" :answer-field/key "$Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "946ddb30-e9a3-52e2-ad46-5aa2c1f15eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73567" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points” is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement “$Ax=0$ has the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f23fb601-4602-5230-a2b6-d601d28b1d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451609} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8e66cf-8fa0-5d72-a062-c894bd90eeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451610} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a8b707-5ade-50d0-9352-ee18ad208d90" :answer-field/key "$det(A)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4599a9d8-85d9-55cd-b0ce-93297e11ff44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451611} {:answer/id #uuid "8805138a-b896-57c2-ae9a-7c7b7fcdcb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451611} :answer-field/id #uuid "261382cf-81eb-57f1-8fd5-08c17079a626" :answer-field/key "The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f312ba01-7f4f-5870-a564-3e53f73eba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451613} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd13e80-555a-5431-b4aa-3bbfe2eec5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451613} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9d9259-e497-5632-a846-f4d0b61339a2" :answer-field/key "The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb595552-3588-5923-aa25-072e92fab82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73570" :question/problem "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions” is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect” is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement $det(A)\\ne 0$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07704e84-a62b-539e-a7a9-9dbecb8c57f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339191} {:answer/id #uuid "2acef88e-d265-5321-9d87-36b6d6294965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  has infinite solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339503} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc79a68-bbf8-5fe0-855a-fd6e9042d20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$  has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339530} {:answer/id #uuid "35b1880d-5bcf-5f87-aab9-e854cfe6cc7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339605} {:answer/id #uuid "cf565f35-3ec6-5a68-b70e-523c4f58b527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340972}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339605} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f8fd6e-f821-5446-9212-b9cf60b76d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "975451bc-691b-585b-833f-78b85768cebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73596" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+2y &=1 \\\\ -3x+ky &=-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true: \n\nIf the system's coefficient matrix is invertible then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements. \n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible then $det(A)\\ne 0.$    \n\nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the two lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2.$ \n\nFinally, notice that if $k=-1$ then \n\n$$\ndet\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot (-1)-2(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $A$ is invertible,  then $k\\ne -1.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the system's coefficient matrix is invertible, then the lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f0d947-f7c9-5e28-b318-ebb60ce25335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution for the system $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339794} {:answer/id #uuid "5adce73b-295c-5739-82fa-c3ee4171f952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339955} {:answer/id #uuid "683aa57a-6da0-5e5b-bb49-7a9b14a713df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340058} {:answer/id #uuid "79847df7-1315-5e34-b716-873875e4ee22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340212} {:answer/id #uuid "93f2b389-1ca6-5535-ba05-5e23607eb465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340459} :answer-field/id #uuid "35101e0e-d5c3-5680-82cf-c57e5604d67b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16a72be1-2bd1-5d5b-86b7-6ea0a4390bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-73608" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14477158-11de-5790-b879-bc43c8ca8958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342417} {:answer/id #uuid "2441c22a-b9d8-5652-ae6e-8b99dc23bcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342545} {:answer/id #uuid "364edc20-5721-5b95-b18f-7e2fcb0c7c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342686} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5c501c-a1fa-5e2e-8a3d-da88a08e2ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343745} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfae4a4-bf96-5ad0-813a-3a5b34415980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343911} :answer-field/id #uuid "49407303-e12b-5aac-a9e2-6cef8ca783a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a57d2de-dfa3-5922-be9d-d7417adc9dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75448" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e64603-bb6e-5b0f-a72d-80a8ccc3450d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468118} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5f4f63-4be8-5a5b-b8cd-fa7559af4fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468118} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1db1d1e-60e6-5396-8aa1-ee91b508535f" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a0153d-23de-5108-888f-f2b222f9131f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468120} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd47bee-3079-5cc3-a48e-29d195deb027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468121} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c80179-cb50-56e3-9723-38b39e5ea82c" :answer-field/key "$B=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fa1bed-27f2-56b2-bedb-00ae85826918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468122} {:answer/id #uuid "58093b55-be97-532c-ab81-3f3b5e4b6b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468123}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468122} :answer-field/id #uuid "046de83c-fd46-54bf-b6be-3214402a6cb6" :answer-field/key "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21485ec9-747a-5129-ba2c-794c570827bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-75496" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf41b36-30bd-512a-957e-7a79e4183513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468124} {:answer/id #uuid "9454a215-809e-5191-9a6a-ef7b9461b589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer